program

A program is a sequence of statements executed in order.

let x = 1
let y = 2
print(x + y)
===
statement

One of the statement forms: let, assign, if, while, match, return, or expression.
===
package_stmt

Declares the package name for this module. Must appear at the top of a module file.

package "mylib"
===
let_stmt

Binds a value to a name, or defines a shape.

let count = 0
let name = "aiki"
let @point [x, y]
let @person [name, @point]
===
field

Field in a shape definition. Either a name or embedded shape.

let @point [x, y]
let @rect [@point, width, height]
===
assign_stmt

Assigns a new value to an existing binding. Errors if name not found.

let x = 1
x = 2
x = x + 1
===
if_stmt

Conditional execution. Guard clause style preferred over nesting.

if x < 0 {
    return :negative
}
if x > 0 {
    return :positive
}
return :zero
===
while_stmt

Loop while condition is true. Use higher-order functions when possible.

let i = 0
while i < 10 {
    print(i)
    i = i + 1
}
===
match_stmt

Pattern match on values. Exhaustive by convention.

match result {
    [@ok, val] {
        print(val)
    }
    [@error, msg] {
        print("error: " + msg)
    }
    _ {
        print("unknown")
    }
}
===
pattern

Pattern for matching: wildcard, name, literal, list, or shaped list.

match x {
    _ { print("any") }
    n { print(n) }
    42 { print("forty-two") }
    [a, b] { print(a + b) }
    [@point, x, y] { print(x) }
}
===
return_stmt

Returns a value from a function. Required in every code path.

let abs = (n) {
    if n < 0 {
        return -n
    }
    return n
}
===
expr_stmt

Expression as statement. Value discarded.

print("hello")
f()
===
expr

Any expression.
===
pipe_expr

Left-to-right pipeline. Left becomes first argument of right.

data |> filter(is_valid) |> map(transform) |> each(print)

Errors short-circuit:

read_file("x.txt") |> parse() |> validate()
===
infix_expr

Left-to-right binary operations. No precedence.

1 + 2 * 3      # equals 9, not 7
(1 + 2) * 3    # equals 9
1 + (2 * 3)    # equals 7
===
unary_expr

Prefix unary operators: not, -

not true       # false
-42            # negative forty-two
===
postfix_expr

Call, index, or field access chained after primary.

f()
list[0]
point.x
data[i].name()
===
call

Function call with arguments.

print("hello")
add(1, 2)
f()
===
index

List or string index. Zero-based.

list[0]
str[i]
===
access

Shaped list field access.

point.x
person.name
===
primary

Literal, name, or grouped expression.

42
"hello"
'r'
:symbol
my_var
true
[]
(a + b)
===
list_literal

Raw or shaped list.

let nums = [1, 2, 3]
let point = [@point, 10, 20]
let empty = []
===
func_literal

Anonymous function. Same syntax as named.

let add = (a, b) { return a + b }
map(nums, (x) { return x * 2 })
===
params

Function parameters. Optional rest parameter.

(a, b)
(a, b, ...rest)
(...args)
===
param_list

List of parameter names.

a, b, c
===
rest_param

Rest parameter collects remaining arguments.

let sum = (...nums) {
    return reduce(nums, 0, (acc, n) { return acc + n })
}
sum(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)
===
block

Block of statements in braces.

{
    let x = 1
    print(x)
}
===
literal

Literal value: number, string, rune, symbol, boolean.

42
"hello"
'a'
:ok
true
===
BINOP

Binary operator. All same precedence, left-to-right.

+ - * / < > <= >= and or
===
NUMBER

Integer, decimal, or rational. Exact arithmetic.

42
3.14
1/3
===
STRING

Double-quoted text.

"hello"
"line one\nline two"
===
RUNE

Single character in single quotes.

'a'
'\n'
'\t'
===
SYMBOL

Colon-prefixed atomic identifier. Compared by identity.

:ok
:error
:point
===
SHAPE

At-prefixed shape name.

@point
@error
@person
===
NAME

Identifier in snake_case.

foo
my_var
_internal
===
WHITESPACE

Spaces, tabs, and newlines. Ignored between tokens.
===
COMMENT

Text from # to end of line. Ignored.

# this is a comment
===
KEYWORD

Reserved words: let, if, else, while, match, return, true, false, and, or, not.
===
OPERATOR

Operators: + - * / < > <= >= |> . =
===
DELIMITER

Punctuation: ( ) [ ] { } , ; ...
===
